# The duty cycle every supply files, or the typed reason it files none. One row per nameplate: how
# much of a period the supply is actually available for.
#
# A second axis of divisibility
#   A second axis of divisibility and not a third value of the first. A supply can arrive in whole
#   units and be intermittent in time at once (a truck that comes twice a week is both), so the
#   amount question and the calendar question are separate columns.
#
# A blank would mean three things
#   A blank would mean three things at once, which is why it is typed. Only two of the three are
#   absences. The first is a value; filed as `absent/reason = none`, it loses both its size and
#   its origin.
#     one whole period   the supply runs continuously. A duty fraction of one, filed as a
#                        value because that is what it is: `1 week` against a period of
#                        `week`, carrying an origin saying who could shorten it.
#     `notApplicable`    the unit has no denominator, so "five days of what?" is malformed.
#     `unmeasured`       nobody asked.
#   Only the last is arithmetically dangerous, and only because a time slack cannot be derived
#   from a clearance when nobody knows whether the spare is spread evenly.
#
# No stated amount required
#   This relation deliberately does not require a stated nameplate amount. The window is a
#   statement about the calendar; a supply nobody has sized still has one.
-- pm:Nameplate/pm:Divisibility/pm:window, beside the amount unit that decides if it is answerable.
SELECT n.filing, n.layer,
       n.window_low, n.window_mode, n.window_high, n.window_unit, n.window_size_absent,
       n.window_absent,
       n.amount_unit
FROM pm.nameplate n
